2. Run New Human School

Extract the ZIP and run this with Node.js 20 or later:

cd new-human-school
node bin/nhs.mjs -i

That starts a small Kindergarten session.

To install the nhs command under your home directory:

npm install --global --offline --prefix "$HOME/.local" .
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"

The core has no npm dependencies. The installation above uses the local project.

Then:

# West: the first eight Paleo letters.
nhs -i --track west --grade k --learn 8 --limit 8

# East: practice ten questions from the first twenty RTK entries.
nhs -i --track east --grade 1 --learn 20 --limit 10

# West: begin the grammar cards in order.
nhs -i --track west --grade 2 --learn 12 --ordered

--learn N chooses the ordered set first. Randomization happens afterward. Thus “learn the first twenty” does not accidentally turn into “draw twenty from the entire book.”

The initial East grade size is 100 RTK frames:

Grade	RTK frames

Kindergarten	Primitives and selected foundational characters
1	1–100
2	101–200
3	201–300
…	…


The importer can rebuild those groupings with another value of N. They are NHS learning groups, not claims about school ages.

Your pipeline works

One Markdown fact, one terminal question:

cat curriculum/west/kindergarten/0001-letter-aleph.md | nhs -i

A directory, shuffled by the shell, with one quiz process per file:

dir=curriculum/east/grade-01

find "$dir" -type f -name '*.md' | sort -R |
  while IFS= read -r path; do
    cat -- "$path" | nhs -i
  done

The implementation reads Markdown from stdin and answers from the controlling terminal. I tested that with actual pseudo-terminal sessions, including filenames containing spaces.

For the GUI side:

find "$dir" -type f -name '*.md' | sort -R |
  while IFS= read -r path; do
    cat -- "$path" | nhs --el
  done > lesson.jsonl

Or the shorter equivalent:

nhs --el --track east --grade 1 --learn 20 --shuffle curriculum > lesson.jsonl

--el emits one versioned JSON record per card. It performs the Markdown-to-GUI-data conversion you described.

