Give me all the Heisig radicals. Not the kangxi radicals. I mean the kangxi radicals plus the things that Heisig had to assign a non-decomposable meaning to (usually derived from its original meaning) in order to "Burn Math Class"-ify the Joyo Kanji. I.e., in order to never force you to memorize anything EXCEPT the "radicals" without the ability to decompose it, he sometimes had to append to the list of primitives. Give me all of those, in approximate Heisig order using RTK1 and RTK3 as the reference for how to order them, and using RTH1 and RTH2 as a backup reference to check the Kanji meanings against. Rule of thumb: We're not trying to learn Chinese or Japanese. We're trying to find the correct way to teach ANY human mind The East aka the Semantic Writing half of the world. So "the alphabet" over there is the radicals. And Thing Explainer for the East (aka the most common 1000 words) is the Joyo Kanji (which is 2000 but still, close enough.)

